1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Hyukgeose Geoseogan (赫(爀) 居世 居西干, 69 BC - 4 AD, reigned: 57 BC - 4 AD) was the first king of Silla.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is the progenitor of the Shilla Park clan. He is commonly referred to as Park Hyukgeose (朴赫居世). He united the forces of the 6 Saro village chiefs to establish the Saro country (斯盧國) and laid the foundation for the nation.
2 CONT [Naver Encyclopedia of Knowledge] Hyukgeosegeeogan [赫居世居西干] (Encyclopedia of Korean National Culture, Central Research Institute of Korean Studies). Hyeokgeose of Silla
2 CONT Monarch: Hyeokgeose of Silla, also known by his personal full name as Bak Hyeokgeose, was the founding monarch of Silla, one of the Three Kingdoms of Korea. He was the progenitor of all Bak clans in Korea. Wikipedia
2 CONT Born: 69 BC
2 CONT Died: 4 AD
2 CONT Spouse: Lady Aryeong
2 CONT Parents: Lady Saso
2 CONT
2 CONT birth
2 CONT Samguk Yusa
2 CONT According to the legend told by the Samguk Yusa, Hyukgeose was born from an egg laid by a white horse that came down from heaven during a meeting between the village chiefs of the 6th division of Saro-guk. In other words, in 69 B.
2 CONC C., the chiefs of six villages gathered together with their children on Alcheon Hill and said, “We do not have a king to rule over all of us, so we are all comfy and we are all doing what we want, so there is no order at all. So, 
2 CONC let's find a virtuous person and make him a king, and let's build a nation." But then, at the foot of a mountain called Yangsan, not far south of the meeting place, Alcheon Hill, a strange energy like lightning appeared. The villa
2 CONC ge chiefs went up to a higher place to get a better view. At the foot of Yangsan, near a well called Najeong (蘿井), there was lightning flashing, and next to it was a white horse kneeling down for a long time as if bowing down befo
2 CONC re making a long sound. He cried and flew into the sky. When the horse left, the village chiefs rushed to the place where the horse was lying, and found a large purple egg lying there. When the village chiefs were looking at the e
2 CONC gg, it suddenly broke. There was a boy who was very neat and beautiful in appearance. Everyone was amazed and amazed, and took the baby to a spring called Dongcheon to wash himself. Then the baby's body shone, and the beasts came 
2 CONC and sang and danced together, and the sky and the earth shook, and the light of the sun and moon grew brighter. The village chiefs said that the child's egg was very large and resembled her gourd, so they named her Pak (朴) and nam
2 CONC ed it Hyukgeose (赫 (爀) 居世).
2 CONT
2 CONT It is also recorded that Il-yeon was born to Mrs. Saso. Here, Our Lady of Narrative is the same goddess as the Leading Mother.[1] Although it is not known exactly where Madame Saso came from, it is said that Hyeongsan, where she s
2 CONC ettled, is a mountain also called Seohyeongsan (西兄山) and Seondosan (仙桃山). . There is also the view that China is not the current China, but literally China (China), that is, a central country that governs various princes.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT There are no records related to her father in Hyukgeose.
2 CONT
2 CONT *The story of Na-jeong's revolution
2 CONT For the legend or tale about the Silla Hyukgeosege Geoseogan, see Silla Hyukgeosege Geoseogan.
2 CONT She built a palace at the western foot of Namsan (now Changrimsa Temple) and raised two sacred children. Her baby boy came out of her egg, and the egg looked like gourd. So people called her Pak (朴), so she called her surname Pak 
2 CONC (朴). Her little girl named her (Najeong) after the well she came from. When the two sages reached the age of 13, in the first year of Obong, in the first year of Obong (57 B.C. The name of the country is called Seorabeol (徐羅伐) or 
2 CONC Seobeol (徐伐) (this is why the current custom calls 'Gyeong' to 'Seobol'), or Sara (斯羅) or Saro (斯盧). Also called At first, because the queen was born in Gyeseong (雞井), it was also called Gyerimguk (雞林國) because Gyeryong showed aus
2 CONC piciousness. However, according to another theory, the name of the country was changed and the name of the country was changed to Gyerim because a chicken crowed in the forest when Kim Al-ji (金閼智) was obtained at the time of Talha
2 CONC e Isa-geum. In the later generations, it was finally decided as Silla.
2 CONT
2 CONT *between
2 CONT The record says the following:
2 CONT
2 CONT At the age of 10, Hyukgeose was exceptionally outstanding and matured, and at the age of 13, at the age of 13, he was crowned as Geoseogan (居 西 干) and the national name was Seorabeol. did.
2 CONT In 41 BC (17th year of accession), he served as a member of the six divisions, followed by his queen, First Lady Al. He encouraged the efforts of farming and herding of silkworms so that all the benefits of the land could be obtai
2 CONC ned.
2 CONT In the spring of 39 BC (19th year of accession), there is a record that Byeonhan (卞HAN) surrendered his country to surrender, but Shin Chae-ho did not trust it.[3]
2 CONT A fortress was built in the capital city of Venus in 37 BC (21 years of accession), and a palace was built in Venus in 32 BC (26 years of accession), and the foundation of the nation was laid at this time.
2 CONT In 28 B.C., Nakrang invaded, but it was called the country of morality and retreated by itself.
2 CONT In the spring of 20 BC (38th year of accession), in the second lunar month of the lunar calendar, he sent an envoy Hogong to Mahan, who blamed King Mahan for not paying tribute. It was released at the persuasion of the gods. And t
2 CONC he following year, King Mahan died, and his servants encouraged him to subdue Mahan, but Hyukgeose said, “It is absurd to think of other people’s misfortunes as a fluke.” So he sent an envoy to pay tribute. This means that Silla's
2 CONC  political system was corrected at that time and was not bound to Mahan.
2 CONT There is a record that an envoy from Dongokjeo (東沃沮) came and offered 20 horses in 5 BC (53rd year of accession), but Shin Chae-ho did not trust this.[3]
2 CONT Dead
2 CONT According to the legend, Geoseogan Hyukgeose ascended to heaven after 62 years of reign, and after 7 days his body was divided into parts and scattered and fell to the ground. The people tried to bury their divided bodies together
2 CONC  again, but a large snake appeared and drove them out and disturbed them. The people were forced to bury their legs, arms, torso, and face separately, resulting in 5 tombs between the Geoseogans of Hyokgeose and the tombs of the G
2 CONC eoseos in the Hyukgeoses, and came to be called Oreung (五陵). It is also called Sareung (巳陵) because the body of the Geoseogan during the Hyukgeose era is buried separately because of the snake. The Orung (五陵) is located to the nor
2 CONC th of Damamsa Temple.
2 CONT
2 CONT *Difference from the Park family genealogy
2 CONT The genealogy of the Park clan of Silla records the eldest son, Park Teuk (朴忒), the eldest son of Geoseogan, Hyukgeose, as the foundation of Silla [4], providing a basis for inferring that the birth of Geoseogan of Hyokgeose is ea
2 CONC rlier than the records of historical sources (69 BC). However, considering the fact that various existing Bohak materials related to the Park clan are not retroactive to the middle or late Joseon Dynasty, the content of the Samguk
2 CONC  Sagi compiled in the 12th century and the Samguk Yusa compiled in the 13th century are viewed as orthodoxy.
2 CONT
2 CONT *between
2 CONT Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
2 CONT Chinese Imperial Mian, Dingling.jpg
2 CONT
2 CONT Geoseogan (居西干) is a title used to refer to the king of the early Silla Dynasty. It is also written as rough (居瑟邯). It is also said to mean 'king' or 'noble person' in Jinjin, and it is said to mean 'commander' or 'priest'.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is an opinion that this title has nothing to do with the fact that the king was sent from Mahan to Jinan. The Chinese character of Geoseogan means 'the king who lives in the west', but from Jinhan's point of view, Mahan is t
2 CONC he west, and the title Geoseogan is also related to the tradition of sending kings from Mahan. On the other hand, this interpretation cannot be completely trusted because Geoseogan is not written according to the meaning of Chines
2 CONC e characters, but is a secondary transcription of the native language of Jin by borrowing Chinese characters with similar pronunciation (that is why there is a separate notation with similar sounds like 居瑟邯). There is also the pos
2 CONC ition that there is no.
2 CONT
2 CONT The time when Park Hyukgeose became king of Silla was the time when Silla began to establish a centralized dynasty system after breaking out of the small state of Jinhan, and it was a time when it built an independent power away f
2 CONC rom the influence of Mahan. In other words, the name Geoseogan can be seen as an expression that reflects the desire to escape from Mahan's influence.
2 CONT
2 CONT The only king who used geoseogan was Park Hyukgeose and Geoseogan, and later titles were changed to Cha Cha-woong-Isa-geum-Maripgan, etc.
