1 NOTE {geni:about_me} King Seonghan
2 CONT Sunghan (Silla)
2 CONT Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
2 CONT Seonghan (? ~ ?) or Kim Seong-han (金星HAN) is believed to be the progenitor of the Kim dynasty of Silla, and is also known as King Seonghan or King Taejo. He also appears as Se Han (勢 Han), Eleven (熱 Han), etc. It does not appear i
2 CONC n historical documents after Goryeo such as 《Samguksagi》 and 《Samguk Yusa》, but appears as the progenitor of the Kim dynasty of Silla in the stone monument and the Geumseokmun. The mausoleum is Taejo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although he does not appear in the Samguk Sagi or Samguk Yusa, according to the tombstones and Geumseokmun of the Silla period, he is said to be the 15th generation of King Munmu and Kim Inmun, and the 24th generation of King Heun
2 CONC gdeok. In addition, the tombstones of the monks Jin-cheol and Jin-cheol of the late Silla Dynasty refer to him as the founder. Chusa Kim Jeong-hee, a calligrapher of the late Joseon Dynasty, also assumed that a person called Seong
2 CONC -han or Se-han and Kim Al-ji were the same person.
2 CONT
2 CONT expression
2 CONT The surname (星) = Kim (金), Han (Han) = Al <閼>", 王 (king) = Ji <智>" to match the surname (星) = King Seonghan, and Alji Kim <金閼智>. There is an opinion that denies the same person.
2 CONT
2 CONT career
2 CONT The exact year of his birth is not known, and the exact date of his birth is not known. According to the Pobeoptapbi, the Great Master Pobeopbi, built in 939, it was called Kiseongangjaseonghan (其先降自seonghan), meaning that the anc
2 CONC estors of Seonghan came from somewhere. Seonghan is a figure appearing in the tombstone of King Munmu, the tombstone of Kim Inmun, the tombstone of King Heungdeok, the inscription of the Great Master Jincheol (眞澈大師塔碑文), the vacuum
2 CONC  pagoda and the sarcophagus of Heungbeopsa Temple in Wonju, etc. According to the tombstone and the geumseokmun, Seonghan appears as the progenitor of the Kim dynasty of Silla, and is sometimes called Taejo (太祖). Unlike the Samguk
2 CONC  Sagi and Samguk Yusa, which describe Kim Al-ji as the progenitor of the Kim clan, not Sunghan, in the Geumseokmun, Kim Al-ji does not appear and refers to Seonghan as the progenitor of the Kim clan. The Silla Kim clan called Seon
2 CONC ghan Taejo instead of Park Hyukgeose, and Kim Bu-sik described him as the son of Kim Al-ji in the Samguk Sagi, but the Silla contemporaries regarded Seonghan as the progenitor. Accordingly, there have been many claims about the id
2 CONC entity of Sunghan.
2 CONT
2 CONT It does not appear in historical documents such as Samguk sagi and Samguk yusa, but Samguk sagi and Samguk yusa were compiled after the Goryeo period.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the tombstone of King Munmu, the tombstone of Kim Inmun, the inscription of the Great Master Jincheol, the vacuum pagoda and sarcophagus of Heungbeopsa Temple in Wonju, and the inscription of the tomb of King Heungdeok, the sur
2 CONC name is the progenitor of the Kim family. In the tomb of King Munmu, Seonghan is called Taejo, and King Seonghan is said to be the 15th generation of King Munmu [1]. In the tomb of King Heungdeok, Seonghan is called Taejo, while K
2 CONC ing Heungdeok is his 24th descendant. In the tombstone of Kim In-mun, it appears as King Taejo Han.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the other hand, the genealogy of the Gyeongju Kim clan or the Silla Kim clan, the Samguk Sagi, and the Michuwang section of the Samguk Yusa only briefly contain only that the last post of Sehan or Seonghan was Yi Chan.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the genealogy of the Pan-Silla Kim clan published after the Joseon Dynasty, which is a genealogical data whose reliability is somewhat unclear, his final official position appears as Lee Chan (伊二 + 倉).
2 CONT
2 CONT It is not known why he honored the tomb of Taejo to his 4th generation, Seonghan, and not to Michu Isageum.
2 CONT
2 CONT In general, the view that Seong-han was the same person as Kim Al-ji[2], the view that Sehan or Eleven Han appeared as the son of Al-ji in the Samguk Sagi and the Samguk Yusa [3], the 7th generation descendant of Al-ji Some view i
2 CONC t as King Michu (味鄒王) who ascended the throne for the first time among the Kim clan [4]. In Gyeongju Kim clan's records, it is recorded as Sehan, and it is said that there are other names such as Eleven Han, Seonghan, and Seonghan
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT On the other hand, according to the inscription of King Munmu, it is presumed that Seonghan is related to Kim Il-je after the war. In some cases, “Tuhu (秺侯) Jecheon Jiyun (祭天之胤) handed down seven generations” (line 5), and “the 15
2 CONC th generation, King Seonghan, whose foundation fell from heaven” (line 6) Interpreting the verse as directly related to it, the 7th-generation descendants of Kim Il-je after fighting are sometimes criticized as Seonghan. Some hist
2 CONC orians claim that the descendants of Kim Il-je were defeated in China and migrated to Silla, and they also introduce the detailed process.[5] However, in the historical circles, records related to Seonghan and Kim Il-jae are regar
2 CONC ded as the product of the ideological ancestral consciousness of the Silla royal family. In the pre-modern era, in order to sanctify the family line, ancient legendary kings or famous great men were often regarded as progenitors, 
2 CONC so it is difficult to believe that the descent from Kim Il-jae is true.[6][7]
2 CONT
2 CONT The date, date, and place of death are unknown, and Neungji and Shiho are also unknown. After his death, the royal family of the Kim clan of Silla called him Taejo, and it is unknown for what reason he was honored as king.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family Relations
2 CONT According to the Silla Kim clan's Seonwon World Map, the Samguk Sagi, and the Simguk Yusa, King Heungdeok is his 21st-generation descendant. However, according to the tombstone of the tomb of King Heungdeok, King Heungdeok is the 
2 CONC 24th generation descendant of Seonghan. King Munmu is the 15th-generation descendant of the Silla Kim clan, Seonwonsaegido, Samguksagi, and Simguk Yusa records, and appears as 15th-generation descendant in the inscriptions of King
2 CONC  Munmu's tomb.
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Alji Kim
2 CONT Descendants: Michu Lee Geum
2 CONT Descendants: Moving money
2 CONT 15th generation: King Munmu
2 CONT 24th generation: King Heungdeok
