1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Xiao hou 蕭后 (Empress Xiao), ca. 570–post 630, Empress of Emperor Yang of Sui (r. 605–617)
2 CONT
2 CONT Empress Xiao was the principal wife of Yang Guang 楊廣 (569–618), second emperor of the Sui dynasty. Her personal name is not known. She was the daughter of Xiao Kui 蕭巋 (542–585), Emperor Ming 明 of Later Liang (r. 562–585), and the 
2 CONC sister of Xiao Cong 蕭琮 (d. 607). She was the great-grandaughter of Xiao Tong 蕭統 (501–531), compiler of the ''Wen xuan''. She was born in the second month, which was considered inauspicious, and thus she was given to her uncle Xiao
2 CONC  Ji 蕭岌 (d. 566), the sixth son  of Xiao Cha 蕭詧 (519–562), to be raised in his family. In 555, Xiao Cha was installed as the puppet emperor of the Western Wei in the regime called Later Liang. After Xiao Cha died in 562, Lady Xiao 
2 CONC was raised by her maternal uncle Zhang Ke 張軻 (n.d.). In 583 Yang Jian 楊堅 (541–604), the Sui Emperor Wen (r. 581–604), was choosing a wife for his son Yang Guang, the Prince of Jin. He wished to select someone from the cultured Xia
2 CONC o family. Xiao Kui recommended his daughter, who was still living in the household of Zhang Ke. The girl impressed both Emperor Wen and his Xianbei wife Empress Dugu 獨孤 (552–602) with her gentility, learning, writing ability, and 
2 CONC knowledge of divination. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When Yang Guang became emperor in 605, he named Lady Xiao his empress. She always accompanied him on his excursions, especially to the southern city of Jiangdu 江都 (modern Yangzhou). Concerned about Yang Guang's profligate ways, Em
2 CONC press Xiao admonished him several times, but her advice was not heeded. She then composed the “Shu zhi fu” 述志賦 (''Fu'' recounting my aims) in which she indirectly expressed her concerns about the dangers the emperor's conduct pose
2 CONC d for the security of the state and the Sui ruling house. One line, “I wish to find comfort in a small space,” was inspired by a line in Tao Qian's “Gui qulai ci” 歸去來辭 (Let me return), thus indicating that she may have shared her 
2 CONC great-grandfather Xiao Tong's interest in Tao Qian's poetry. 
2 CONT
2 CONT By the year 616, Yang Guang had lost much support of his officials and generals. He took his entourage south to Jiangdu. Empress Xiao was aware that members of the Sui court were planning to assassinate Yang Guang. A palace lady v
2 CONC olunteered to report this news to the emperor, who immediately had her executed. From this time on, Empress Xiao did not venture to convey any more warnings to her husband. In 618, the Sui general Yuwen Huaji 宇文化及 (d. 619) killed 
2 CONC Yang Guang. He took Empress Xiao north, but he was soon killed by a force led by Dou Jiande 竇建德 (573–621). Empress Xiao then took up residence in Luozhou 洛州 (northeast of Luoyang). About this time the Sui Princess of Yicheng 義城, w
2 CONC ho had married the chieftan of the Turkic Eastern Tujue 突厥 (Göktürks), the Qimin 啟民 Qaghan (r. 599–611), sought to have Empress Xiao released from her captivity with Dou Jiande. Qimin's son, the Chuluo 處羅 Qaghan (r. 619–620), sent
2 CONC  an emissary to bring Empress Xiao to live in the Tujue court. She remained with the Tujue until 630, when the Tang defeated the Tujue. Empress Xiao took up residence in Chang'an where she died. The date of her death is not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
