0 @N6000000002188099910@ NOTE Wu Zetian
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Wu Zetian 
1 CONT Birth and death: 625¹–December 16, 705 
1 CONT Family name: Wu (?) 
1 CONT Given name: Mei² (?), later Zhao³ (?) 
1 CONT Dates of reign: Oct. 19, 6904–Feb. 22, 7055 
1 CONT Temple name: None6 
1 CONT Posthumous name:
1 CONT
1 CONT (short) Empress Zetian7 (????) 
1 CONT Posthumous name:
1 CONT
1 CONT (full) Empress Zetian Shunsheng8
1 CONT ?????? 
1 CONT General note: Dates given here are in the Julian calendar.
1 CONT They are not in the proleptic Gregorian calendar.
1 CONT ———
1 CONT 1. Year deduced from the age at death given in the
1 CONT New Book of Tang (???), compiled in 1045-1060, which
1 CONT is the year favored by modern historians. The year of birth
1 CONT deduced from the Book of Tang (???), compiled in
1 CONT 941-945, is 623. The year of birth deducted from the
1 CONT Comprehensive Mirror compiled in 1065-84, is 624.
1 CONT 2. Was given this name by Emperor Taizong in the late 630s
1 CONT after she had entered the imperial palace (see inside article).
1 CONT 3. Had this Chinese character created in December 689 and
1 CONT chose it as her given name. Became her taboo name when she
1 CONT ascended the throne the next year. Some sources assert that
1 CONT this character was actually written ? . Some sources also
1 CONT assert that her original given name was Zhao (?) , and that
1 CONT in 689 she only changed the character to write her name, but
1 CONT this is confirmed neither by the Book of Tang, nor by the
1 CONT New Book of Tang, which both do not record her original
1 CONT given name, if she had any.
1 CONT 4. Was already partially in control of power since around
1 CONT 660, and totally since January 665 (see inside article). Zhou
1 CONT dynasty was proclaimed on October 16, 690, and she
1 CONT proclaimed herself emperor on October 19, demoting her
1 CONT younger son Emperor Ruizong to the rank of imperial heir.
1 CONT 5. Lost power at the palace coup of February 20, 705. Then
1 CONT on February 22 was forced to transfer the imperial dignity
1 CONT onto her older son, restored as Emperor Zhongzong
1 CONT on February 23
1 CONT 6. Zhou dynasty was abolished before her death, and she
1 CONT was reverted to the rank of empress consort on her death, so
1 CONT that she does not have a temple name. In China, empresses
1 CONT consort, unlike ruling emperors, are not given a temple name.
1 CONT 7. Zetian was the beginning of the honorific name (??) 
1 CONT given to her in February 705 by her son the restored emperor
1 CONT Zhongzong. The honorific name was used as her posthumous
1 CONT name when she died ten months later.
1 CONT 8. Final version of her posthumous name as given in July 749. 
1 CONT Wu Zetian (??? pinyin : Wu Zétian) (625 - December 16, 705), personal name Wu Zhao (??), was the only woman in the history of China to assume the title of Emperor (Huangdi; ??). Ruling China first through puppet emperors from 665 
1 CONC to 690, which wasn't unprecedented in Chinese history, she then broke all precedents when she founded her own dynasty in 690, the Zhou (?), and ruled personally under the name Emperor Shengshen (????) from 690 to 705. Her rise and
1 CONC  reign has been criticized harshly by Confucian historians but has been viewed under a different light after the 1950s.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Birth 
1 CONT 2 Road to power 
1 CONT 3 Rule 
1 CONT 4 Evaluation 
1 CONT 5 Zhou Dynasty (690 - 705) 
1 CONT 6 See also 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Birth
1 CONT Her family was from Wenshui (??), part of Bingzhou (??) prefecture (now called Taiyuan in Shanxi province). Wenshui is now a county (???) inside the prefecture-level city of Lüliang (???) and located 80 km.(50 miles) southwest of 
1 CONC Taiyuan. Her father was Wu Shihuo (???) (577-635), a member of a renowned aristocratic family of Shanxi, and an ally of Li Yuan, the founder of the Tang Dynasty, in his conquest of power (Li Yuan was himself from a renowned aristo
1 CONC cratic family of Shanxi). Her mother was Lady Yang (??) (579-670), a woman from the Sui imperial family. Wu Zetian was not born in Wenshui, as her father was a high ranking civil servant serving in various posts and locations alon
1 CONC g his life. The most serious claimant for her birth place is Lizhou (??) prefecture, now the prefecture-level city of Guangyuan (???), in the north of Sichuan province, some 800 km (500 miles) southwest of Wenshui, but other place
1 CONC s have been proposed, including the capital Chang'an.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Road to power
1 CONT She entered Emperor Taizong's harem most probably in 638 (other possible date: 636), and was made a cairen (??), i.e. one of the nine concubines of the fifth rank. Emperor Taizong gave her the name Mei (?), meaning "charming, beau
1 CONC tiful". Thus, today Chinese people refer to her as Wu Meiniang (???, i.e. "Miss Wu Mei") when they write about her youth, whereas they refer to her as Wu Zetian (???) or as Empress Wu (??) when they write about her time in power.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 649, Taizong died, and as was customary for concubines Wu Meiniang had to leave the imperial palace and enter a Buddhist nunnery where she had her hair shaved. Not long afterwards, most probably in 651, she was reintegrated int
1 CONC o the imperial palace by Emperor Gaozong, son of Taizong, who had been struck by her beauty while visiting and worshipping in the nunnery. Gaozong's empress consort, from the Wang (?) family, played a key role in the reintegration
1 CONC  of Wu Meiniang in the imperial palace. The emperor at the time was greatly attached to a concubine from the Xiao (?) family, and the empress hoped that the arrival of a new beautiful concubine would divert the emperor from the co
1 CONC ncubine née Xiao. Modern historians dispute this traditional history, and some think that the young Wu Zetian never actually left the imperial palace, and that she was probably already having an affair with the crown prince (who b
1 CONC ecame Emperor Gaozong) while Emperor Taizong was still alive. Wherever the truth lies, it remains certain that by the early 650s Wu Zetian was a concubine of Emperor Gaozong, and she was titled zhaoyi (??), i.e. the highest rankin
1 CONC g of the nine concubines of the second rank. Wu Zetian soon had the concubine née Xiao out of the way. The fact that the emperor had taken one of the concubines of his father as his own concubine, and what's more a nun, if traditi
1 CONC onal history is to be believed, was found to be utterly shocking by Confucian moralists.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the year 654, Wu Zetian's baby daughter was killed. Empress Wang was allegedly seen near the child's room by eyewitnesses. She was suspected of killing the girl out of jealousy and was persecuted. Legend has it that Wu Zetian a
1 CONC ctually killed her own daughter, but the allegation may have been made up by her opponents or by Confucian historians. Soon after that, she succeeded in having the emperor create for her the extraordinary title of chenfei (??), wh
1 CONC ich ranked her above the four concubines of the first rank and immediately below the empress consort. Then eventually, in November 655, the empress née Wang was demoted and Wu Zetian was made empress consort. Wu later had Wang and
1 CONC  Xiao executed in a cruel manner -- their arms and legs were battered and broken, and then they were put in large wine urns and left to die after several days of agony.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Rule
1 CONT After Emperor Gaozong started to suffer from strokes from November 660 on, she began to govern China from behind the scenes. She was even more in absolute control of power after she had Shangguan Yi (???) executed and the demoted 
1 CONC crown prince Li Zhong (??) forced to commit suicide in January 665, and henceforth she sat behind to the now silent emperor during court audiences (most probably, she sat behind a screen at the rear of the throne) and took decisio
1 CONC ns. She reigned in his name and then, after his death, in the name of subsequent puppet emperors (her son Emperor Zhongzong and then her younger son Emperor Ruizong), only assuming power herself in October 690, when she proclaimed
1 CONC  the Zhou Dynasty, named after her father's nominal posthumous fief as well as in reference to the illustrious Zhou Dynasty of ancient Chinese history from which she claimed the Wu family was descended. In December 689, ten months
1 CONC  before she officially ascended the throne, she had the government create the character Zhao (?), an entirely new invention, created along with 11 other characters in order to show her absolute power, and she chose this new charac
1 CONC ter as her given name, which became her taboo name when she ascended the throne ten months later. The character is made up of 2 pre-existing characters: "Ming" up top meaning "light" or "clearness"; and "kong" on the bottom meanin
1 CONC g "sky". The idea behind this is the implication that she is like the light shining from the sky. Even the pronunciation of the new character is exactly the same as "shine" in Chinese. On ascending the throne, she proclaimed herse
1 CONC lf Emperor Shengshen, the first woman ever to use the title emperor (??) which had been created 900 years before by the first emperor of China Qin Shi Huang. Indeed she was the only woman in the 2100 years of imperial China ever t
1 CONC o use the title emperor and to sit on the throne (instead of merely ruling from behind the throne), and this again utterly shocked Confucian elites.
1 CONT
1 CONT Traditional Chinese political theory (see the similar Salic law) did not allow a woman to ascend the throne, and Empress Wu was determined to quash the opposition and promote loyal officials within the bureaucracy. Her regime was 
1 CONC characterized by Machiavellian cleverness and brutal despotism. During her reign, she formed her own Secret Police to deal with any opposition that might arise. She was also supported by her two lovers, the Zhang brothers (Zhang Y
1 CONC izhi, ???, and his younger brother Zhang Changzong ???). She gained popular support by advocating Buddhism but ruthlessly persecuted her opponents within the royal family and the nobility. In October 695, after several additions o
1 CONC f characters, her imperial name was definitely set as Emperor Tiance Jinlun Shengshen (????????), a name which did not undergo further changes until the end of her reign.
1 CONT
